WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:05.227
During World War II, Canada built over four hundred warships in under five years.

00:00:05.227 --> 00:00:10.000
In the modern era, Canada has failed to deliver a single combat ship in thirty years.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.159
The Canadian Surface Combatant program was baselined at twenty-six point two billion dollars.

00:00:15.159 --> 00:00:17.863
Current estimates exceed eighty-four billion.

00:00:17.863 --> 00:00:23.079
The F-35 lifecycle cost: twenty-seven point seven billion dollars.

00:00:23.079 --> 00:00:28.193
The Auditor General has flagged over ten billion in understatements across procurement programs.

00:00:28.193 --> 00:00:31.568
Over one hundred billion dollars in failed military procurement.

00:00:31.568 --> 00:00:40.306
The pattern is consistent across every major program: unrealistic timelines, ballooning costs, sole-source contracts, and zero accountability.

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:47.727
The Canadian Armed Forces cannot field the equipment it needs because the procurement system is designed to distribute contracts, not deliver capability.

00:00:47.727 --> 00:00:49.295
The troops pay the price.

00:00:49.295 --> 00:00:51.295
The contractors collect the cheques.
